's R-leaning fundamentals (R+5 PVI) and incumbency cut against a big Democratic edge"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican-leaning seat with an incumbent advantage, but every available poll shows Poindexter ahead, prediction markets price him near 80%, and Miller is damaged by abuse allegations and negative outside advertising. That combination justifies a modest Democratic edge beyond a pure tossup, though the polling is thin and dated.", "watch": "Fresh independent polls in October, or a shift in the abuse-allegation story, could move this back to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Max Miller (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ohio's 7th District was drawn to favor Republicans, and incumbent Max Miller began the cycle with the usual advantages of incumbency and a large fundraising lead. But the race has been upended by domestic abuse allegations against Miller, which have drawn national attention and outside spending against him. Challenger Brian Poindexter has raised over $600,000 and benefits from roughly $770,000 in outside support, while TV stations have aired his ads despite Miller's legal threats.\n\nMost expert raters call the race a Tossup, though two rate it Lean D. The available polls, all from June through early August, show Poindexter ahead by margins ranging from one to fourteen points, with a Democratic-skewed average. Polymarket prices Poindexter near 80 percent. The evidence points to a narrow Democratic edge in a race that could help decide House control, with affordability a central campaign theme.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6543, "margin": 2.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7013, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7273, "margin": 3.5}]}